<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jhalawar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1838 CE – 1948 CE · capital Jhalrapatan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rajput state carved out of Kota in 1838 for the heirs of the regent Zalim Singh Jhala, ruled from Jhalrapatan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalim-singh-jhala">Zalim Singh Jhala</a> was the powerful regent of Kota in the late 18th and early 19th centuries, and his descendants held the real power there. To end the rivalry between them and the Kota ruler, the British in 1838 created a separate state from part of Kota for his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madan-singh-of-jhalawar">Madan Singh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jhalawar was a 13-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a>, ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhala">Jhala</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> Maharaj Rana. Its capital lay beside the old temple town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhalrapatan">Jhalrapatan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhawani-singh-of-jhalawar">Bhawani Singh</a> (ruled 1899–1929) was known for his learning and interest in education, and later rulers continued to build schools and libraries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Jhalawar acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and in 1948 was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a>; it is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jhalawar-state.jpg" alt="Jhalawar State" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jhalawar.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1838 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jhalrapatan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Madan Singh, Bhawani Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hadoti, Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jhalawar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=jhalawar-state" aria-label="Jhalawar State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6703 1272 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jhalawar State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7023 1528L7010 1507M7023 1528L7364 1601M7023 1528L7364 1602M7023 1528L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7010 1507h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7023 1528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7023 1528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=jhalawar-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
